::GEO_U03_L02_GP_Q01::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L02_GP_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.2<br><br>Question:<br>A triangle has angles 48&deg; and 67&deg;. What is the third angle? {
=A. 65&deg;#Correct. 48 + 67 = 115, and 180 - 115 = 65.
~B. 55&deg;#This subtracts incorrectly from 180&deg;. Recheck the angle sum.
~C. 75&deg;#This does not make the three angles total 180&deg;.
~D. 115&deg;#This is the sum of the two given angles, not the missing angle.
}

::GEO_U03_L02_GP_Q02::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L02_GP_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.2<br><br>Question:<br>A triangle has angles 2x&deg;, 3x&deg;, and 4x&deg;. What is x? {
~A. 18#This would make the angle sum 162&deg;, not 180&deg;.
=B. 20#Correct. 2x + 3x + 4x = 180, so 9x = 180 and x = 20.
~C. 30#This would make the angle sum 270&deg;.
~D. 45#This would make the angle sum 405&deg;.
}

::GEO_U03_L02_GP_Q03::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L02_GP_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.2<br><br>Question:<br>An exterior angle of a triangle is 110&deg;. One remote interior angle is 45&deg;. What is the other remote interior angle? {
~A. 55&deg;#This does not add with 45&deg; to make the exterior angle.
~B. 70&deg;#This would make the remote interior sum 115&deg;.
=C. 65&deg;#Correct. By the Exterior Angle Theorem, 45 + x = 110, so x = 65.
~D. 155&deg;#This adds the exterior angle and remote angle instead of subtracting.
}

::GEO_U03_L02_GP_Q04::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L02_GP_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which theorem explains why the interior angles of a triangle add to 180&deg;? {
~A. Exterior Angle Theorem#The Exterior Angle Theorem relates an exterior angle to two remote interior angles.
~B. Vertical Angles Theorem#Vertical Angles Theorem describes opposite angles formed by intersecting lines.
~C. Pythagorean Theorem#The Pythagorean Theorem relates side lengths in right triangles.
=D. Triangle Angle Sum Theorem#Correct. The Triangle Angle Sum Theorem states that triangle interior angles sum to 180&deg;.
}

::GEO_U03_L02_GP_Q05::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L02_GP_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.2<br><br>Question:<br>An exterior angle is formed at one vertex. Which angles are remote interior angles? {
=A. The two interior angles not adjacent to the exterior angle.#Correct. Remote interior angles are the two nonadjacent interior angles.
~B. The exterior angle and its adjacent interior angle.#The adjacent interior angle is not remote.
~C. Only the largest angle in the triangle.#Remote interior angles are based on position, not size.
~D. Any two angles that look equal.#Appearance does not define remote interior angles.
}

